title: Waiting a tick
mode: draw
chapter: Memory
check: behaves
parts: dff
---- intro
A flip-flop's output can feed another flip-flop. At each tick, the second one copies what the first one **was** holding, just before the first one changes. So a value moves one flip-flop further at every tick.

Set `d` to 1 and press **Step**: `q1` lights. Press **Step** again: now `q2` lights too, one tick later.
---- task
Chain two flip-flops: `d` into the first, the first into the second. `q1` shows the first, `q2` the second.
---- start
input d 0 0
clock clk 0 140
led q1 440 20
led q2 440 110
---- reference
input d
clock clk
led q1, q2
q1 = dff(d, clk)
q2 = dff(q1, clk)
---- answer
input d 0 0
clock clk 0 140
dff f1 140 20
dff f2 300 100
led q1 440 20
led q2 440 110
d -> f1.d
clk -> f1.clk
f1.q -> f2.d
clk -> f2.clk
f1.q -> q1
f2.q -> q2
---- hint1
Both flip-flops get the same clock, `clk`.
---- hint2
The first flip-flop's `q` goes to `q1` and to the second flip-flop's `d`. The second one's `q` goes to `q2`.
---- solution
`d` into flip-flop 1, its `q` into flip-flop 2 and to `q1`; flip-flop 2's `q` to `q2`; `clk` to both. Whatever `d` was shows on `q2` two ticks later.
---- done
A value marching from flip-flop to flip-flop is a **shift register**. Processors use the same idea to work on several instructions at once, one step apart: a **pipeline**.
